<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annual Customs of Dahomey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xwetanu, Huetanu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Annual Customs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> were the main yearly celebration of the Kingdom of Dahomey, held at its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a>, and largely begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">King Agaja</a> around 1730. Every family lineage was expected to bring gifts and tribute to the king, who displayed the wealth and then redistributed much of it. The ceremony honoured the royal ancestors with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>: captives from war and criminals were killed, around 500 at a time, mostly by decapitation, and as many as 4,000 were reported killed in 1727. The Customs also held military parades and the Great Council, where leaders, women among them, debated policy until the king declared consensus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annual-customs-of-dahomey.jpg" alt="Annual Customs of Dahomey" width="330" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archibad Dalzel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victims_for_sacrifice-1793.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
